{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And their meal-offering and their libations.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Ta'anit: concerning the second day it says ונסכיהם, and concerning the sixth day ונסכיה, and concerning the seventh day כמשפטם — mem, yod, mem: behold here 'water' (מים). From this our Rabbis of blessed memory based a hint for the water libation on the Festival." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to understand how the hint of the waters begins from the second day: for on the second day the waters were separated in the act of Creation, and therefore the action of the second day is great in waters. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the men of the ma'amad would fast on the second day because of those who descend the waters (i.e., the rainy season). And likewise on the sixth day, on which man was created and needed water, as it is written (Genesis 2:6): ״ואד יעלה מן הארץ״, and it is written (ibid.): ״וייצר ה' אלהים״. And He completed the hint on the seventh, because the seventh is the completion of the act of Creation. And they said in tractate Rosh HaShanah: the Holy One, blessed be He, said: pour out before Me water on the Festival, so that the rains of the year will be blessed for you. Now all seven days of the Festival they would pour the water upon the altar, and this is a halakhah given to Moses at Sinai. And together with the wine libation of the morning's daily offering he would pour the water separately, at the southwestern corner, above the midpoint of the altar, and all of it would flow down into the shittin. And how would they do it? A golden flask holding three log would he fill from the waters of Shiloah. When they reached the Water Gate they sounded the shofar — teki'ah, teru'ah, teki'ah. He would go up the ramp, turn to his left, and pour the water from the flask into the bowl that was there. And two silver bowls were there: the western one held water, the eastern one held wine. They were pierced with two thin snout-like holes, and the hole of the water bowl was finer than that of the wine, so that the water and the wine would run out together. To the one pouring the water they would say: 'Raise your hand!' — for once one poured it over his feet, and all the people pelted him with their etrogim, saying: 'He is a minim!' — for the minim say that the water libation is not done. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'One who did not see the rejoicing of the House of the Water-Drawing has never seen rejoicing in his days.' And it is called the House of the Water-Drawing (בית השואבה) on account of (Isaiah 12:3): ״ושאבתם מים בששון״ — for they would draw water at night all seven days for the needs of the daily offerings."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:56.600952412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}